speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, it may be helpful to the house to survey briefly the work remaining to be done before the summer adjournment. There are some items on the order paper that should take relatively little house time because they are either not highly controversial or they have already reached an advanced stage. In this category I have listed the following items:
Item No. 62 with regard to the Maritimes Freight Rates Act amendment;
Item No. 63 with respect to the Export Development Corporation bill;
Item No. 64 with respect to the "Telesat Canada" bill;

Item No. 65 with regard to the Canada Shipping Act amendment bill;
Item No. 66 with respect to the oil and gas production and conservation bill;
Item No. 77 with respect to the Patent Act amendment bill;
Item No. 78 with respect to the Fisheries Improvement Loans Act amendment bill; and
Item No. 79 with respect to the Criminal Code off-track betting amendment bill.
In addition there are other items on which the house has already invested considerable time or for which we feel there is urgency. This list includes the following items:
Item No. 4 regarding the official languages bill;
Item No. 73 with respect to the income tax amendment bill; and
Item Nos. 75 and 82 regarding the two bills amending the National Housing Act, which are at the moment before the house.
[DOT] (2:40 p.m.)
If the Minister of Finance gives notice of any tax changes tomorrow, those changes will have to be dealt with during this session.
In addition, the Minister of Regional Economic Expansion has introduced his bill relating to incentives for regional development. Understandably, he wants it to be dealt with just as soon as possible.
Then I believe we should assume that the Standing Committee on Procedure and Organization will be making a report, pursuant to the order of the house made on December 20 last, relating to the allocation of time to legislative business.
Against this small mountain of work we must set the number of days available. As hon. members know, Friday, June 27 is regarded as the best target date for the adjournment. Quite aside from the convenience of members-a consideration which we all share-there are practical reasons relating to the preparation of the legislative work for the next session which make it highly desirable that the house should not continue into July or meet again too early at the end of the summer, if this is at all possible.
Members will understand that the ministry wants to have July relatively free for concentrated work on policy questions relating to the bills for the next session, and that we should like to have most of September for the detailed examination of draft bills. This does not mean that some of the standing committees should not meet in September; indeed,
June 2, 1969

there is much to recommend such a course of action where a committee has been given a suitable reference by the house.
The immediate problem is that including today and Friday, June 27, in the calculation, an adjournment on the 27th would allow only 19 more sitting days. Of these a total of 14 are preempted under the Standing Orders: eight are supply days on which opposition motions will be debated and six are for the debate on the budget statement. This means that unless some extra days can be found, after today there will be only four days for government legislative business, if the target date for the adjournment is not to be overrun.
In consultations which already have taken place I have put forward for consideration various ways in which the house could reconcile the work to be done with the time available. These consultations will be continuing; however, I thought all members should know just how things stand.
